      "writtenOn": "2026-02-05T18:18:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You raise valid points `There should be nothing particularly interesting about the \"family\" at this point`. I observed the specific Chromium use case that motivated this change.\n\nThe Issue:\nIn Chromium\u0027s font_cache_skia_win.cc, after creating a typeface, there\u0027s validation logic in `TypefacesMatchesFamily()` that calls `tf-\u003egetFamilyName()` to verify the typeface actually matches the requested family:\n\nThis is used in CreateFontPlatformData() to reject typefaces that don\u0027t match the requested family name - preventing font substitution from going undetected.\n\nWhat is happening for system variable fonts via FontDataService:\n- Browser process matches font (e.g.,\"Segoe UI Variable Display\") and sends variation coordinates + other info.\n- In MakeFromStream, we search the stream collection for a matching font.\n- fontFace is correctly recreated with variations applied via apply_fontargument_variation().\n- But the font/fontFamily objects passed to DWriteFontTypeface::Make() were still from the original search result (based on ttc_index).\n- onGetFamilyName() uses fDWriteFontFamily-\u003eGetFamilyNames(), returning the wrong family in some cases.\n\nWhy GetFontFromFontFace helps:\n- After CreateFontFace() with new axis values, the resulting fontFace corresponds to a specific named instance in the collection.\n- GetFontFromFontFace() finds the IDWriteFont that matches this varied face, which then gives us the correct IDWriteFontFamily with accurate family names.\n\nYou\u0027re right that ideally Chromium shouldn\u0027t rely on family names from data-backed typefaces for matching. However, it seems this validation exists to handle Windows font substitution via registry entries.",
      "parentUuid": "f0990def_799118e1",
